{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Esav said, I have enough, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary for him to say <b>my brother</b> after [the word] — since he was speaking with him [directly], and moreover he should have placed it at the beginning of the utterance, not in the middle of the words." "<b>Now</b> Esav's intention follows this path: if you are aiming for my benefit — <b>I have enough</b> and I do not need this. And if you are aiming for your own benefit, in order that my heart draw close to you, as he revealed by saying ״to find favor״ — <b>let what you have be to me, my brother</b>, etc., meaning: my love for you, which is rooted in brotherhood, will not be recognized unless you take back your gift; for without this it will appear that my behaving toward you with brotherhood is only for the sake of the gift. And this is the verse's delimitation: <b>my brother</b> — meaning, it will be recognizable that I hold you as a brother if [what you have] is to me, etc." "<b>Please, I pray, if now I have found, etc.</b> One needs to know why he lengthened by saying <b>na</b> two times?" "<b>Now</b> the verse's intention follows this path: he comes to respond that his words are true — that a measure of brotherhood would be more recognizable if he does not take anything from him. But when was this so? Before he brought [the gift]; but not now, after he has already brought him the gift — it is not fitting to return it to him. And that is his saying <b>please (na)</b>, meaning: now, after I have brought you the gift, <b>if now (na)</b> I have found favor and you love me as a brother loves a brother, you will add to do kindness with me by taking my gift from my hand. Therefore he was precise to say <b>and take</b> with the addition of the vav. And his saying <b>for therefore</b>, meaning: that the manner of the gift should be not to conduct myself with you in brotherhood but rather according to the custom of those who receive great men's presence, that they do not see their faces empty. Therefore you need to consent to take my gift, for without this it would proclaim the opposite of [my] desire and affection." "<b>And if</b> for the concern that [Jacob taking the gift] would not diminish from Jacob's wealth, therefore he said ״for Elohim has been gracious to me and I have all.״ The meaning of his saying <b>all</b> — perhaps he intends to say that the gift he brought him does not break a breach in his possessions, and after he has given him what he gave him as a gift, his possessions remain as they were beforehand, all of them in completeness without lack. Understand this." "<b>Further</b>, he alludes in saying <b>kol</b> — that this is the dimension of holiness called ״kol״ (all). When this dimension is grasped within a person, any lack makes no impression, and what is missing fills itself in automatically — as in the blessing of Israel who is like sand, where what is missing fills itself in; this is the blessing of ״kol.״ This is the sod of (I Kings 17:16) ״כַּד הַקֶּמַח לֹא כָלָתָה״ (the jar of meal did not run out). See what I explained on the verse (Genesis 33:18) ״וַיָּבֹא יַעֲקֹב שָׁלֵם.״ This blessing was attained by all the patriarchs: Abraham — ba-kol (with everything); Isaac — mi-kol (from everything); Jacob — kol (everything) (Bava Batra 47a). And this is its sod. From this one can now give a good reason for his saying ״קַח נָא אֶת בִּרְכָתִי״ and so on, ״כִּי חַנַּנִי אֱלֹהִים וְכִי יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ — for what is missing fills itself in automatically."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {5 "The phrase כַּחוֹל (״like sand״) is read here as an allusion to HaShem's promise to Israel that their seed would be as the sand of the sea, which cannot be counted and thus never appears diminished."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:03.025679609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}